# Deferred work

- Partial setup failure leaks Vulkan objects: if `Renderer::new` fails after
  the swapchain exists (e.g. a `Frame::new` call fails), the swapchain and any
  already-created frames are never destroyed, and neither are the pieces of a
  half-built `Frame` or `Swapchain`. Fix with the deletion queue, so every
  created object is registered for destruction as soon as it exists.
- Split depth ranges: one near plane can't serve both a cockpit 1 m away and a
  planet 10,000 km away (reverse-Z depth below ~1e-8 fails the depth test).
  Render the far scene with a large near plane, clear depth, then render
  nearby objects with a small one, in the same frame. Needed once vessels
  exist; until then the near plane follows the camera's altitude.
- The tonemap pipeline is built for the swapchain's color format. If a
  swapchain recreation ever picks a different format (e.g. the window moves to
  a monitor with other surface formats), it no longer matches; rebuild it on
  recreation when the format changes.
- Distant-body markers: bodies smaller than a pixel are no longer drawn at
  all (the fixed-brightness dots were removed). Redesign how distant bodies
  are shown, e.g. as physically bright points, map-mode icons or labels.
- Pack files: unknown nodes are rejected, but unknown properties are not, so a
  misspelled optional property (e.g. a rate) is silently ignored. Reject
  properties a node doesn't define.
- system-solar data accuracy against JPL Horizons (fitted mean elements, see
  `crates/packs/tests/system_solar.rs`): planets and most moons within ~0.1° as seen
  from their parent. Weak fits: Mimas drifts several degrees and Tethys ~0.3°
  (their resonance swings Mimas ±44° over 70 years), Hyperion up to 15° (4:3
  resonance with Titan), Phoebe ~1°, Pluto's small moons 1–2° (they orbit a
  binary), Ryugu, Pallas and Psyche 0.2–0.3° today. Mimas, Tethys and the
  asteroids are fitted over 2016–2036, everything else over 2000–2050;
  accuracy degrades outside those spans. Dactyl's orbit comes from Sol's
  config (Horizons has none). The Moon's mean elements omit its periodic
  terms (up to ~2° along its orbit) and are referred to the equinox of date
  (~0.36° offset by 2026). Spin poles are exact at J2000 but their drift (up
  to ~0.1° per century) is not representable; moons spin with their axis
  along their orbit normal; bodies without an IAU rotation model spin
  untilted with Sol's period (Sol's own behavior). Vesta, Eros, Ida and
  Phoebe rotation values and Arrokoth's 9 km radius should be checked
  against their sources.
- Simulation time treats UTC as TDB (Barycentric Dynamical Time), ignoring a
  ~69 s offset: Io sits ~1,200 km and the Moon ~70 km behind NASA's live
  positions. Fix with a leap-second table in `time.rs`.
- Every body texture is uploaded when joining a simulation (~1.1 GB of VRAM
  with Sol's 4k set), one at a time with a full queue wait each. Fine at 4k;
  the 16k set needs texture streaming with a VRAM budget.
- Revisit auto-exposure: no approach so far is satisfying. In place: an
  image-based meter (luminance histogram) that classifies pixels as dark
  (below 0.1 cd/m²), lit, or star surface (above 10⁷ cd/m²) and exposes for
  the darkest of the average lit surface at mid-grey, lit highlights just
  under white, and a visible star within a 14-stop sensor range. Known
  problems: the Sun flickers as the camera moves (its disk overflows
  RGBA16F, so its measured brightness depends on the exposure and flips
  between classes); the Sun plus a crescent darkens the crescent (the
  14-stop rule); stars only show in full darkness (physically expected). An
  incident-light meter (exposure from the illuminance at the camera, with
  body shadows) was tried side by side and did not satisfy either. Ideas:
  treat saturated pixels as a lower bound (star surface, never lit); meter
  the subject and let light sources clip; identify star disks by a ray-
  sphere test instead of luminance (also keeps sun glints on metal or water
  and bright plumes from counting as stars); local exposure (eye-like
  regional adaptation) for starfields beside bright bodies; the Sun's glare
  as its own effect. Revisit once terrain, vessels, plumes, atmospheres and
  planetshine exist, and decide with the scenes side by side.
- The client loads shaders from the `base` pack only, before joining, so other
  packs cannot override them yet (part of custom shaders in packs).
- A remote client must have the server's packs installed with matching
  versions; streaming them is part of multiplayer.

- Calendar durations: `time step` takes `s`, `min`, `h` and `t` only. Days,
  weeks and years are calendar units relative to a home body, so `d`, `w` and
  `y` need a calendar defined by the simulation: declared outright (Sol uses
  86,400 s days and 365.25-day Julian years) or derived from a home body
  (solar day from its rotation and orbital periods, year from its orbit).
- Message permissions: any connected client, remote ones included, may change
  the time rate, set or step time, and join as a console. Needs roles checked
  by the server and rate limiting, since every accepted change is broadcast to
  all clients.
- Distances accept `m`, `km`, `Mm` and `Gm`; astronomical units (`au`) are not
  supported.

- Clock synchronization: a client's time is the last tick's server time plus
  elapsed time × rate, so a remote client runs one-way latency × rate behind
  the server (500 s at 10,000× with 50 ms). Undecided until vessel physics and
  multiplayer exist; candidates: QUIC's smoothed RTT estimate with slewed
  corrections, a fixed playback delay, or scheduling every time-critical
  action at an absolute simulation time so the offset is display-only.
- The Windows IPC path (named pipes in `crates/commands/src/ipc.rs`) and terminal
  restore (`crates/commands/src/terminal.rs`) have never been compiled; no Windows
  target is installed.

# Next steps

- Translation keys (undecided): explicit keys in files following
  `<pack>.description`, `<pack>.<module>.<entry>.<field>` and
  `<pack>.ui.<screen>.<name>`, or keys derived implicitly from ids.
- Asset hosting: publish binary pack resources as a versioned `.tar.zst`
  archive per pack (players open it with 7-Zip 24.01+ or Windows 11; we create
  it with our own tooling) in S3, optionally behind CloudFront for download
  costs. Repository keeps pack text files only; fetch-assets downloads the
  latest release and extracts the binaries into the working tree (first
  release bootstrapped by hand); a release task packs and uploads, later a
  GitHub Action. Upload credentials through fnox.
- Slang ↔ Rust layout check: the `shaders` task also writes
  `slangc -reflection-json` output; a client build script reads it
  (serde_json as a build dependency) and generates offsets and sizes that Rust
  const assertions compare field by field.
- Grow the per-frame material buffer like the object buffer instead of failing
  past a fixed capacity.
- Handle types (ObjectHandle, MaterialHandle, CubeMapHandle) use a named
  `index` field instead of tuple structs.
- Theme text styles beyond `title` and `telemetry`, each added when a view
  first needs it: `display` (main menu, 48/600), `heading` (sections,
  17/600), `body` (paragraphs, 15/400), `label` (controls, 13/500), `caption`
  (secondary, 11/400), `overline` (category tags, 11/600, uppercase, +0.08 em)
  and `code` (console, `code` typeface 13/400).
- xui environment: increased contrast, a UI-scale setting that multiplies
  every size, and a color-role modifier reading the theme. `TextCase` from
  text styles is applied once `Text` has a case modifier.
- Interface roadmap, in order, each its own pull request (design in
  `docs/xui.md`):
  1. Navigation: xui `ViewController`, `NavigationController`,
     `NavigationView`, transitions with a delegate, `.opacity`, `.offset`,
     `.hidden`; xsa view controllers (main menu with CONTINUE, LOAD, NEW GAME,
     OPTIONS, QUIT; config and load placeholders; game with the HUD; the
     pause menu), `ui` commands, the `Actions` handle, link-style buttons and
     a `menu` text style, the Escape and F3 binds replacing every debug bind,
     the mouse and pause rules.
  2. Main menu presentation: time frozen in the menu, the spawn body framed
     from its lit side on the right, a cosmetic spin from its rotation rate,
     and a zoom transition on CONTINUE.
  3. xui controls (`Toggle`, `Picker`, `Slider`) and a graphics config panel
     (stars, bloom, tonemapper, exposure mode) that runs typed config
     commands through the executor, no command text parsing.
  Later: `ScrollView`, `List`, keybind editing in the config panel. Saves,
  switching worlds behind a loading screen and the menu showing the latest
  save's spawn body wait until the foundations for saving exist.
- xui: skip the bodies whose inputs did not change, as SwiftUI does, once
  re-running every body each frame shows up in profiles. Needs comparable
  views or tracking which state and environment values each body read.
- xui glyph atlas: evict or grow instead of failing once its 1024² texture is
  full.
- Before extracting xui into `xui-rs`: its own error type instead of anyhow,
  and a test font of its own instead of reading Inter from xsa's `data/`.

# Planned features


- Real solar system at scale: bodies generated from real physical and orbital
  parameters. The general shape must be right; fine detail may be procedural.
- Planet textures from Sol (RSS-Reborn, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, license shipped in
  `data/system-solar/LICENSE`). Keep the texture pipeline source-agnostic so
  they can be swapped for NASA-derived data before any commercial release.
  Used: Color (alpha is a water mask on Earth), Normal, Height, Emissive (Io),
  Scatter and Surge (Hapke parameters), VertexHeight (terrain, goes to
  `data/`). Ignored: Dummy, Icon, Biomes, VertexColor. Order: textured bodies
  first, then HDR, Hapke shading, and only later rings, atmospheres, clouds,
  the Sun's corona and flares. Texture conventions (confirmed from the
  AdvancedPQSTools, Parallax and Kopernicus sources): DDS rows are uploaded
  as stored, first row is the south pole (v = 0); u = 0.25 − longitude/360°
  (prime meridian at u = 0.25, u grows westward), v = 0.5 + latitude/180°.
  Normal maps are tangent-space RG: R toward west (+u), G toward north,
  z = sqrt(1 − x² − y²). Color is sRGB; Normal, Height, Scatter, Surge and
  Emissive are linear. Shading is Custom/HapkeScaled from CharonSSS/
  AdvancedPQSTools (MIT, port with its notice): Scatter R = w, G = b,
  B = (c + 1)/2; Surge R = B_S0/2, G = h_S, B = B_C0/2, A = h_C.
- Anti-aliasing: TAA built in-house. It must handle geometric edges (planet
  limbs), shader aliasing (normal-mapped detail, glints), sub-pixel objects
  and noise from ray-marched effects: camera jitter, per-body motion vectors,
  neighborhood color clamping, sharpening, history reset on time-warp jumps
  and target switches; distant-body markers and orbit lines drawn after it.
  Lands after bloom and before atmospheres and clouds, which depend on
  temporal accumulation; gets a toggle key. Later, optionally: AMD FSR 2/3
  (MIT, vendor-neutral) as a quality and upscaling mode on the same inputs.
  No motion blur: motion vectors exist only for TAA and FSR.
- Spawn locations: `spawn` in a simulation names only a body. It will need
  more detail (where on or around the body: a launch site, a surface
  position, an orbit), or become a points-of-interest system where several
  places, such as space stations, can be chosen as the starting point.
- Lens effects for the Sun: diffraction spikes and lens-flare ghosts, drawn
  as their own post-processing effect with a toggle key (Sol ships flare,
  spike and ghost textures). Kept subtle; separate from bloom, which only
  models the lens's soft scatter.
- Stars from a catalog: render stars as points from a star catalog (Hipparcos
  or Tycho-2, or Gaia for millions) with physical brightness from their
  measured magnitudes, pinpoint sharp at any zoom, over a dimmer Milky Way
  texture for the diffuse glow. Keeps the physically calibrated sky.
- Commands, the remaining phases (the router, REPL, IPC and time and camera
  commands exist):
  - `ui [<ui-id>] show|hide|toggle` once there is a UI; a snap without the
    UI is `ui hide`, `camera snap`, `ui show`, so the snap itself never
    takes a UI option. Vessels, maneuvers, resources, EVA, SAS and RCS
    commands with gameplay.
- Rings as data: inner/outer radii and orientation in the system data
  (literature values; Sol's ring radii use an unknown unit), textures as
  resources.
- Ground detail textures: Sol ships its own only for Earth, Luna and Mars;
  other bodies reference stock KSP textures that cannot be redistributed.
  Investigate Sol's set and build similar textures for the rest.
- Texture streaming and a VRAM budget: textures are not all preloaded — only
  the mip levels a texture needs at its current on-screen size are resident,
  loaded and evicted in the background, and total usage stays under the budget
  reported by VK_EXT_memory_budget (least recently used evicted first).
  Required before high-resolution texture packs.
- Planet LOD: cube-sphere faces split into quadtrees as the camera approaches;
  each tile has its own local origin to keep f32 precision on the surface.
  Once terrain displaces vertices, culling bounds must include the maximum
  elevation (a body's `bounding_radius`, then each tile's own min/max height),
  or peaks reaching into view are culled; horizon culling joins frustum culling.
- Seamless map mode: the map is the live world zoomed out. The camera stays
  centered on the current target (no automatic re-targeting); map overlays
  (orbits, markers) fade in only once zoomed out far enough to need them.
- Camera modes:
  - Debug: creative-style fly camera — WASD horizontal, Space/Shift up/down,
    scroll changes speed, mouse captured.
  - Game: locked on a target — hold right click to capture the cursor and
    orbit, scroll zooms in and out.
- Time control: pause and warp rates limited by situation (in atmosphere, in
  orbit, eventually interstellar). Rewind is out of scope for performance
  reasons; if ever added, debug mode only.
- Game modes:
  - Survival: career with gradual unlocks and a realistic feel.
  - Creative: survival with infinite resources.
  - Debug: cheats and dev tools — edit positions, orbits and other state.
    The mode used to build the game, also available to players.
- Sky grid debug overlay (to think through): right ascension lines every 1h,
  declination lines every 10°, celestial equator and ecliptic highlighted,
  pole markers — drawn procedurally in the skybox shader.
- HDR rendering with auto-exposure and bloom: stars near the Sun become hard
  to see through exposure and glare (not light pollution — space has none);
  also replaces the fixed dot brightness compromise.
- Multiplayer (single player comes first): time warp across players
  (subspaces), latency with client interpolation and prediction, bandwidth
  (bodies on rails need no streaming), streaming a server's packs to
  connecting clients with consent and safety checks.
- Interstellar travel: Voronoi regions assign space to star systems; vessels
  use positions relative to their system, since f64 resolves only ~8 m at
  interstellar distances.
- AI-assisted translation of lang files through a mise task; English is the
  reference language.
- Effects system (plumes, explosions, particles); atmosphere and cloud visuals
  tie into it.
- Custom shaders in packs: packs ship SPIR-V compiled against the shader
  interface published by `base` (shared module, data layouts); shaders declare
  their material properties in a sidecar so materials set them by name, which
  replaces the closed Material enum. Interface changes bump the pack format.
- 3D model formats: glTF 2.0 preferred; support whatever formats the chosen
  open-source models use.
- Simulation step: planets on rails — Keplerian orbits with barycenters, and
  precomputed ephemerides (e.g. JPL DE440) for real bodies, both pure
  functions of time. Vessels use restricted n-body (planets pull vessels, not
  the reverse) limited to significant bodies, with symplectic integrators for
  coasting, adaptive steps for burns and flybys, and Encke's method (integrate
  the deviation from a Kepler orbit) for large steps. A coasting vessel's
  trajectory is computed once and treated as rails until it changes — cheap
  time warp and the map-mode orbit prediction. Any live n-body between bodies
  stays server-authoritative with periodic keyframes, since floating point
  differs across machines.
